    scope: "Only two sentences: 'Those reasons can be checked independently of who offered them.' in the closing paragraph (narrow to the factual reasons; the weighing is criticizable, not verifiable) and 'The compatibility cost falls on identifiable callers' in the paragraph after the 3.11 table (narrow to losses a team can find by testing dependencies before moving)."
  - pass: readability-and-flow
    scope: "Whole body except the 'What Commonplace chooses to count' section (heading, both paragraphs), the bolded investigation-rule sentence, and the closing paragraph. Add one general sentence after the opening Python 2/3 example stating both halves of the claim, with the reversal rule still scoped to the decision at hand. Move the library-hours case into 'What analysis can settle' as the illustration for 'Who gains, and who bears the loss?', shorten that bullet's gloss to a back-reference, cut 'Asking for another month of counts would miss the disagreement.', fold the transfer gloss into a clause but keep the hypothetical marker and both branches. Replace 'even on the first name' with an unambiguous referent such as the ASCII name; name the AGENTS.md table where 'a longer table' and 'the whole table' appear; delete 'This distinction guides a decision.'"
stop: null
